welcome everyone to the International  Spy Museum we are delighted to have you  here this evening and I think you are in  for a delightful evening my name is  Peter earnest I'm the executive director  of the museum I'm very honored to have  all of you here this evening but there's  one or two guests I would particularly  like to invite you to meet and one and  it's such a pleasure because it is a  rare visit not all that common I would  like you to meet the founder of the  International Spy Museum  Milt mauls and his wife Tamar who is the  chairman of our board none of this would  have happened if mild' hadn't had the  idea he served in NSA as a young man and  had the idea of doing something like  this for many years and he did it the  other one I would like you to meet is a  member of our board former chief of  counterintelligence for the Soviet KGB  general Oleg Kalugin yes  and our guest this evening of course as  you know you have come here is Dame  Stella Rimington Dame Stella Rimington  was spent some 35 years in the British  security service from 1969 to 1996  during that period she served in all  three branches both counter-intel Kenner  espionage counter subversion and  counterterrorism and rose to become head  of each of those staffs she then was the  direct the Deputy Director one of the  deputy directors and following a trip  she made to Moscow the first trip that  the British had made to Moscow to meet  the KGB under other circumstances on a  return she was told that she'd been  promoted to director general of mi5 the  British domestic security service she  was the first woman promoted to that  position she was also the first head of  mi5 to be publicly announced the press  had been trying to get a picture of her  for months well they finally got one  because she posed for it  Dame Remington was a great advocate of  openness and transparency transparency  to the public and openness as to the  nature of the service she was the  proponent of that when she was there and  she continued to do that in her  retirement both in her memoirs her  autobiography open secret as well as  presentations speeches and so forth that  she's made subsequently the year that  she retired 1996 in the Honours List for  that year she was named Dame Stella  Rimington commander order of the bath  and that is a title that she has that is  the DCB to this day in 2004 she  published her first novel at risk her  second one in 2006 secret asset and in  2007 illegal action which is the one  that we have here this evening and will  be available for signing after the  presentation  subsequent to her retirement she served  on several boards one of which was of  course Marks &amp; Spencer which are all  familiar with the BG group and I'm very  very honored to say the board of the  International Spy Museum since 2007 we  are indeed very honored to have her on  the board before naming into the board  we of course did a background check she  did very well  I of course asked my colleagues because  you know there's there's the official  things that are said and there's what  people say around the water fund water  fountain and everybody I know gave her a  real thumbs up and I think what finally  solidified it for me was after I wrote  Dame Stella Remington I said dear Dame  Stella Rimington she wrote back a very  cordial reply and at the end of it she  said call me Stella  please join me in welcoming Stella  Rimington  well good evening everybody I'm  delighted to be here at the  International Spy Museum which i think  is one of the most interesting and  lively museums in town but then i would  say that wouldn't as i'm on the board as  you've heard i'm going to talk about my  experiences of my career in mi5 and  really i'm going to talk about how the  threats that that service faces have  changed and how mi5 had to change as  well to cope with them  I joined mi5 as you've heard in the late  1960s and the fact that I joined at all  was a complete fluke because in those  days nobody knew anything about British  intelligence nobody knew who worked  there nobody knew what it was where it  was and in particular of course nobody  knew how you joined and recruitment like  everything else was a secret as I found  out now the website setting out the sort  of jobs that there are available and you  can apply online and they have job  advertisements appearing in the  newspapers and even on the notice boards  of gyms I hear and surprisingly enough  if you google Russian intelligence  service you get a sponsored link for  jobs in mi5  and I think you probably know there's a  very popular TV series which is called  mi5 here and spooks in the United  Kingdom though I must say that I  wouldn't rely on it too much as a source  of information  I was actually recruited to mi5 in India  I'd given up my first profession which  was as a historical archivist to go with  my husband who is a diplomat and he was  posted to the British High Commission in  New Delhi and there I was cheerfully  doing what diplomats wives did in those  days which I must say was not very much  except going to coffee mornings and  appearing in amateur dramatics when  somebody sidled up to me in the British  High Commission compound and tapped me  on the shoulder and said do you want to  be a spy or something like that anyway  and it was the mi5 man in charge of the  small little sort of department which  they had there in those days and he  recruited me as a part-time member of  mi5 as a clerk typist the only  difficulty was that with a degree as I  had an opposed graduate diploma I  couldn't actually type but that didn't  really seem to matter because in those  days people were recruited really for  who they were rather than what they  could do and of course as a diplomat's  wife I was by definition reliable I  thought she's wonderful I knew nothing  at all about mi5 except that I knew that  it had something to do with spies and  there was a lot of spying going on in  those days by both sides in India it was  one of the four fronts really of the  Cold War and I found this absolutely  fascinating so when we came home I  applied for and got a full-time job in  mi5 the UK has got three intelligence  services for those of you who don't know  there's mi5 mi6 and GCHQ  and they're all civilian none of them  have any police powers or any military  powers and none of them are exactly like  any aspect of US intelligence mi5 the  defensive arm and their job is to defend  the country against major threats to our  national security whatever they may be  and obviously they very  on the state of the world at any given  time when I joined the major threat came  from espionage from the Soviet Union and  it's our lives nowadays of course the  major threat comes from terrorists mi5  works very closely with mi6 which is our  foreign intelligence gathering service  which equates broadly but not totally  with the CIA and then we have GCHQ which  equates much more closely with NSA and  together these three services gather  intelligence from many different sources  at home and abroad by all the classical  intelligence gathering means by  intercepting communications  eavesdropping with covert microphones in  the places where people meet to do their  plotting surveillance physically  following people around but the best  sources even in today's technical world  are human beings the terrorists or the  spy deep in the heart of their  organization who can be persuaded to  change sides stay where they are and  work for you effectively to betray their  colleagues and finding those people and  recruiting them and running them is  still an essential part of intelligence  work then of course you've got carefully  to analyze and assess everything that  you've learned decide if you believe it  go out and get more and take action and  this is the key thing at the right  moment you hope to prevent the bomb  going off or the spy getting the secrets  intelligence will never give you the  whole picture by definition so it's  often extremely difficult to decide what  action to take and when to take it in  the Cold War much action came in the  form of disruption try and identify the  foreign intelligence officer who's  working against you in your country try  and recruit them to your side or if you  can't expose them embarrass them get  them expelled from the country nowadays  when terrorists are the main threat  action is much more likely to be in the  form of arrests but arrests will not be  effective unless you've got evidence  that you can use  in court and often of course  intelligence isn't evidence and you  can't use it in court so the dilemma is  acute if you take action too late the  bomb goes off if you take action too  soon you can't hold your suspects and  they get away and may come back another  day when you haven't got any  intelligence at all when we were working  against the IRA as we were for a good  part of my career we tended to let their  operations run on we watched them as  they plotted and planned and really we  watched them write to the moment when  they were planting their bomb and then  they were arrested and of course there  was plenty of evidence now when my  former colleagues are dealing with  suicide bombers with the possibility of  extremely nasty forms of attack the  risks of doing that are deemed to be too  great and the police feel the need to  arrest on the intelligence of intent and  of planning and then of course they've  got to collect evidence afterwards in  order to justify those arrests and bring  these people to court and that's a  problem and that's partly why in the UK  there is a big debate going on at the  moment as the government is seeking  powers to hold suspects in terrorist  cases longer before charging them than  they have been allowed to do in the past  and the other angle to that of course is  the complexity of the data that's now  having to be analyzed as these cases  often have very big international  connections of all kinds so the big  debate goes on between the government  and the supporters of civil liberties  who feel that these powers are not  necessary and breach are hard-won civil  liberties I joined mi5 at the height of  the Cold War an overwhelming stress of  course in those days was from the Soviet  Union and their East European allies and  their efforts at espionage and  subversion as they tried to spread world  communism and undermine Western  democracies their intelligence services  were present in our capitals in large  numbers masquerading as diplomats  journalists Trade Representatives you  name it really  and in those days it was all rather like  a john lecarre a novel for those of you  who read John the Kerry and know about  George Smiley and his colleagues people  were leaving packets of money and hollow  trees on Hampstead Heath in return for  piles of secret documents our jobs  course was to find out what was going on  and stop it and the way we countered  Cold War espionage was through detailed  investigation often over years of the  spy we got results in those days through  my a minut knowledge of the opposing  intelligence services of their people of  the their modus operandi as we called it  so we followed them around we listened  to their telephones we watched their  headquarters in their embassies in our  capitals and we run double agents  against them to try and find out what  they were up to and disrupt them in  those days speed was not nearly as  important as certainty the mi5 I joined  in those days was a static secretive  inward-looking  organization and that attitude was  partly explained by a well-founded fear  of infiltration  I joined not that long after the  Cambridge spies Sylvie Burgess and  MacLean had been exposed and had fled to  Moscow and there were people in British  intelligence and also in the CIA on this  side who were convinced that the whole  of Western intelligence was infiltrated  and penetrated by the KGB and that was a  sort of conviction I suppose supported  by a series of defectors from the Soviet  Union on my side we had Peter Wright who  famously was tried in Australia for  publishing a book called spy capture and  on your side  you had James Angleton in the CIA and  these two work together to create this  sort of sense of real paranoia and  amazingly not long actually before I  joined this culminated in my service in  an investigation of the then director  general for in case he was a spy on no  grounds whatsoever as far as I can see  well presumably he sat quietly in his  office getting on with his work while  this investigation  taking place around him in those days  international contacts international  intelligence contacts were very limited  for us they were limited to the United  States Canada Australia and New Zealand  the trusted friends of the Cold War  because of the same fear of infiltration  that another service you might be  dealing with might be infiltrated in the  UK contacts between ourselves and  government and the civil service were  very limited we even managed to keep the  Treasury at bay in those days on the  grounds that what we were doing was far  too secret for them to know anything  about you couldn't get away with that  nowadays I can tell you there were no  laws or external oversight mechanisms in  the United Kingdom at that time to  govern what we did for people who worked  in the organisation there were very  strict rules about what you could say  about your work and as far as I can  remember it was almost nothing to  anybody about anything really I do beg  you remember there was one rule that you  were allowed to tell your fiance where  you worked but not your boyfriend and I  don't I don't think that that's the rule  that they could operate nowadays  essentially and you could certainly tell  nobody what you did which of course made  social life extremely difficult  particularly for young people who love  to talk about the wonderful job they've  just got for women looking at it from my  point of view now there was another  problem mi5 in those days was operating  not so much a glass ceiling more of a  glass box with walls all round in which  we women were kept  we were regarded as being ok the desk  work for dealing with the papers for  filing and collating and a little bit of  intelligence analysis if we were thought  to be quite bright but certainly not the  sharp end work and certainly not dealing  with human sources the people the best  sources of intelligence before long not  surprisingly we women who were just as  well educated as the men I would say  were pressing to break out of this box  and in an experiment I was the first  woman who was allowed to go on the  course that was teaching the skills that  we needed to  do this human source recruiting and  running and I thought great this is the  breakthrough women are on their way but  when I got on this course it was quite  clear that thinking had only gone so far  and the course was assuming that all the  students would be men because the first  thing you had to do the first exercise  was that you had to go into a pub and I  didn't using some cover story about who  you were and what you were doing you had  to identify anybody you liked in this  pub and try and find out as much as you  possibly could about them and then  unbeknownst to you somebody from the  course was going to come in and blow  your cover story and the test was how  well you dealt with these two angles of  the exercise and I can remember to this  day I was given my pub which was a very  sleazy sort of dump near a Victoria  Station in London I can remember to this  day going into this pub and standing at  the door and surveying the scene it the  pub was all full of men of course they  all seemed to me to be men and dirty  Macs leaving on the bar drinking pints  of beer as though they would be in those  days and I went up to one of these guys  and started chatting him up and it was  quite clear to me that he was just  beginning to think my purpose was  something quite different from  intelligence cover when the guy from the  course came in and I treated him as a  bit of a savior really which was not  quite what was intended so that was the  start for women for many years though  any of the kind of family-friendly  policies that make it easier for women  particularly women with children to work  were resisted part-time work was  regarded as inconceivable job shares had  never been heard of in those days and it  was thought absolutely vital that all  intelligence officers work 24/7 if the  case required it which led of course to  some complicated experiences for me as  my family grew and I ended up with two  small children and my family life and my  professional I've got pretty intertwined  I can remember once I was talking to a  man from one of the East European  embassies in London who was thinking of  defecting and had arranged to meet this  chap in a safe house because obviously  if his side had known what he was doing  is certainly his liberty  possibly his life would have been at  risk and I was just going off to meet  him when the phone on my desk rang and  it was the nanny to say that my younger  daughter II was a baby at the time had  gone into convulsions she had called an  ambulance and would I meet them at the  hospital so had this classic dilemma  well-known and I think to all working  mothers who are you going to let down on  this occasion I managed not to let  anybody down because the safe house was  quite near the hospital  so I by shared good luck actually so I  rushed off to the safe house I explained  to this chap what had happened I  borrowed money from him for all the  taxis involved in the rushing about and  I got to the hospital in time to see the  doctor that guy never did defect in the  end and I've never known whether it was  the apparent shortage of money available  to British intelligence that actually  put him off now of course all the sort  of flexible employment employment  policies that most reasonable companies  have nowadays are in place and there  have now been two women director  generals but with all the modernization  in the world  I think secret organizations still  present complex people and leadership  issues some of the problems of course  our motivation people don't join things  like mi5 for money because we're talking  here about the public service they  joined through a sense of service of  loyalty excitement the importance of the  job they're doing but their successes  are inevitably hidden and their failures  tend to be there for everybody to see in  the form of dead bodies or broken glass  on the pavement you can't use classic  reward mechanisms money and public  acclaim so instead you've got to  generate something else some sense of  satisfaction in a job well done  operations of course are difficult  require difficult decisions and can have  a very dramatic effect if they go wrong  so you've got to manage people in  smallish groups really to ensure they  don't go wrong but if you're over  supervise people who've joined for those  sort of motives that I've described then  they tend to lose their motivation and  then the security of course everybody  has got secrets in their head so you  require  fairly intrusive security people have  got to keep an eye out for colleagues  behaving strangely perhaps but again you  need mutual trust and loyalty of people  are going to support each other in  difficult situations and then  everybody's private life gets mixed up  with their work in the way I've  described so all that needs pretty  careful handling by an alert strong and  visible leadership to create this sense  of belonging almost of a family really  because it's important that people feel  proud and confident in what they belong  to far more important though than the  social change that let women into the  shop and work was the change in the  threat in the 70s and then increasingly  on into the 80s and the 90s a new threat  was beginning to appear and that was the  threat of terrorism which was going to  change everything about the mi5 that I  joined firstly it came from the  situation in Northern Ireland and the  IRA rapidly became one of the best  resourced and the most professional  terrorist organizations in the world  thanks in large part to Colonel Gaddafi  who provided them with arms and large  quantities of money during the 1980s and  then at the same time we had what we  then called international terrorism  firstly in the form of national groups  like the Red Brigades assorted  Palestinian groups and by the 80s if you  remember those of you who are old enough  there were hijackings kidnappings the  murder of political opponents by some  countries on our streets even by the  time I left mi5 in 1996  75 percent of our work was against  terrorist error ISM though of course  espionage still goes on spying never  ends but the targets change as the world  changes and the focus of Western counter  espionage work nowadays is much more  likely to be Iran perhaps and Syria but  it is Bulgaria Poland and Hungary but of  course some old enmities die hard as  we've seen from the death of Alexander  Litvinenko in London which is widely  assumed  was carried out by the KGB and I read in  the newspapers just before I left London  that my former service is now  complaining that there are as many  Russian intelligence officers in London  now as the where during the Cold War and  that they're having to divert resources  from fighting terrorism to working out  what they're all up to so the world  perhaps doesn't change as much as we  might think it should so going back to  terrorism though September the 11th 2001  seemed to some people like the beginning  of a new era of horror in the world's  history to those of us who'd been  working in the field in the UK against  terrorism for a long time we saw it  really more as a stage possibly the  worst stage so far of a phenomenon that  had gripped the world for more than 30  years when we first faced terrorism we  tried to deal with it in the same way as  we dealt with the Soviet spies but it  soon became clear that countering  terrorism requires a completely  different response good intelligence is  just as important but reliable  intelligence is often very difficult to  obtain there's often lots of what you  might call information circulating  around but you can't take action on  everything you hear or the country and  the world would be brought to a halt  with security alerts times always short  and rapid judgments have got to be made  about what's true and what isn't and  what you can safely ignore and what  you've got to take action on and it's  about balancing risks and it's a  difficult thing to do and it requires  nerve and resolution because the penalty  of getting it wrong is huge  might be loss of life on a large scale  or massive destruction of property and  it will be certain blame as the media  picks over everything that you've done  or haven't done in the quiet aftermath  and judges it it's a very different  thing from the quiet slow secretive  world of counterespionage today's  terrorists I think presents an even more  difficult problem than the ones I'm used  to dealing with when we were combating  the IRA we  knew where they were they were a  structured organization and very soon we  knew who they were we knew what they  wanted they wanted the Brits out of  Northern Ireland so we knew broadly what  their targets would be anything to do  with the British state so they murdered  the military officers they blew up mrs.  Thatcher and her cabinet when they were  having their Party conference and nearly  killed them all and their mortared  number 10 Downing Street when the then  Prime Minister John Major was holding a  cabinet meeting but we also knew what  they would not do the IRA were not  suicide bombers they wanted to escape  with their lives so the worst forms of  attacks chemical biological even nuclear  they would never have contemplated today  terrorists I think are very different  and I believe that the terrorist has  changed even since 9/11 it seems clear  that al Qaeda hasn't only survived the  war on terror but has become almost what  you might describe as a global franchise  with fluid networks of people who are  trained to carry out attacks and can  deliver these centrally planned attacks  often on European streets in particular  at present in the UK there are groups of  young men in our cities in the UK born  and educated there British citizens  who've developed for some reason a deep  hatred for the country that they live in  and its people and they're sympathetic  to and sometimes directly directed by al  Qaeda but they don't belong to  organizations with headquarters like  intelligence services that you can  target in the same way as we did in the  Cold War and their fanaticism makes it  very difficult to recruit sources among  them to get information and they don't  have precise objectives either they're  not interested in political solutions  they just seem intent on killing the  largest number of unconnected people  that they possibly can  workers going to work on a bus or an  underground train or an aeroplane so  it's very hard to predict their targets  it means everybody  target and everybody's got to get  involved in security measures everybody  wants to know what's being done to  protect them and businesses and  individuals as well as the government is  a target terrorism in my opinion sad to  say is here to stay for the foreseeable  future  and I think that the declaration of a  war on terrorism leading as it did to  the war in Iraq and everything that's  followed from that unfortunately has all  fed this fanaticism and strength of  terror we've recently been told in the  UK that mi5 my former service was aware  of 200 networks in the United Kingdom  alone and that they were attempting to  disrupt nearly 30 separate plots to  carry out mass casualty attacks and  that's in spite of everything that's  being done against them there have been  I must say on the other side a number of  successful disruptions the people have  been put on trial thanks to good  intelligence work but however much  resources put in to counterterrorism  there's net it's never going to be  possible to disrupt all these plans and  yesterday of course was the second  anniversary of what we in the UK called  7-7 which was the series of bombs on the  London Underground and I think just to  sum up on this you've only got to  reflect really on a chilling video which  was made by one of those bombers and in  that video he said what you have  witnessed now is only the beginning of a  string of attacks that will continue and  become stronger we are a hundred percent  committed to the cause of Islam we love  death the way you love life and even if  a lot of that is wishful thinking and  bravado it does look as though this is a  phenomenon that we're going to be living  with for quite a long time to come so  the need to tackle terrorism brought all  sorts of changes to us in mi5 and in  particular it required more openness  openness with foreign services to share  intelligence and openness at home with  our courts and with government ministers  I became director of  Counterterrorism just a few days before  Lockerbie when Pan Am 103 was brought  down by a bomb over Lockerbie in  Scotland there was huge pressure on us  for a quick answer and at the same time  the IRA was trying to kill British  soldiers in Germany and then when that  began not to be successful because of  good intelligence they moved to the  streets of Great Britain I used to have  to go regularly across to number 10  Downing Street to talk to the Prime  Minister and update him on the situation  and it wasn't always very satisfactory I  would say this is the beginning of our  openness the sort of thing I sometimes  used to have to say to him was well  Prime Minister we know that the IRA are  about to bring in a large truck bomb we  don't know which port it's coming in at  we don't know when it's coming in and we  don't know what the target is but Prime  Minister I thought you should know it's  not what you want to have to tell the  boss I can tell you but to do him credit  he reacted in exactly the right way he  used to sort of close his eyes and  leaned back in his chair and say oh well  Stiller do your best which was exactly  what we did actually and very often  those bombs didn't go off because we did  manage to get prior intelligence as the  eighties turned into the 90s there was  another massive change for our  intelligence services when all of a  sudden with surprising speed the Cold  War came to an end and people started  saying we don't need those intelligence  services anymore they're all about the  Cold War but we knew we were needed more  than ever because of the threat from  terrorism in an early move we made  contact with our former enemies in the  services of Eastern Europe and we I  found myself sitting in their  headquarters exchanging stories with  them about what we'd been trying to do  to them during the Cold War and as you  can imagine it was a great and very very  exciting time the oddest of all I have  to say was my visit to Moscow in  December 1991 that Peter alluded to to  make our first contact with KGB it came  about because the cotton was the man  whom yeltsin had put in charge of the  KGB and he I believe was a true Democrat  really wanted to change things and he  met the British Foreign Secretary and he  knew that we had just been legislating  for our intelligence services in Britain  and he asked whether we could send over  some people to talk to the KGB about how  you must behave in their democracy and I  went with a couple of colleagues to do  just that and we found ourselves in the  Lubyanka which is their headquarters  almost lecturing a long line of  hard-faced KGB officers on things like  laws and how you must behave in the  democracy it was quite clear to me that  they had absolutely no intention of  behaving in any way differently from the  way they'd always behaved and I think it  was not a meeting of minds in any way  because we saw that as a moment of hope  really a moment when the world could get  together again after the Cold War but I  think they saw it as a moment of failure  and certainly liaison between the two  services never really took off in the  way it did with the East European  services shortly after I got back from  that trip my appointment as director  general was announced and we've been or  I'd been through a lot of change by them  during my working life but I was about  to experience a personal change which  was going to alter my life and that of  my family radically because I was told  at the same time as Peter said almost by  the way that my name was going to be  made public on appointment the  government thought that this was a good  openness announcement and also of course  that it was a good equality announcement  because I was a woman imagine what it's  like to be suddenly thrust into the  limelight after a career in a secret  organisation like everybody I'd created  a kind of carapace a cover of anonymity  and it's still only the name of the  Director General which has made public  if you don't tell people what you do it  makes it very difficult to have the kind  of casual sort of social relationships  which most people take for granted if  you accept your neighbors invitation to  Christmas drinks you have to prepare for  the moment which will inevitably come  when somebody says and what do you do  and you either make  have some story for the evening about  how you buy boots for the army or you  work for a cosmetics company or  something or you rush around the room so  fast that nobody ever has a chance to  talk to you at all which is not very  satisfactory whatever you've decided to  be for the evening though you can  guarantee that the first person you talk  to is going to be an expert in just that  thing so people become weary of social  situations and close friendships tend to  be developed within the ring of secrecy  which is not necessarily a very good  thing either so one day for me that kind  of anonymity turned to fame in a rather  dramatic way the press had this sudden  announcement about a woman in a man's  job as they saw it with no photographs  to go with it because it was at a time  when the IRA was very active and our  tabloids never the most subtle of people  went into freefall in their excitement I  was clearly not James Bond and I didn't  look like smiley should look either so  their first reaction was to try and get  me back where I belonged which was in  the kitchen and we had headlines like  housewife super spy and another effort  was mother of two gets tough with  terrorists they found out quite easily  where we lived because we'd always lived  just ordinarily relying on my anonymity  came and camped outside the house to the  great annoyance of the neighbors who  suddenly realized that this quiet lady  who'd lived on their street for the last  10 years was not what she seemed to be  and presented a risk and eventually when  one newspaper published a photograph of  our house on the front page we had to  move overnight and start to live a fully  Clover to life for our own safety which  as you can imagine put great strains on  my daughter's particularly the younger  one who was then I think she was about  17 still living at home and she then had  to be extremely careful who she invited  to the house and who she could give her  telephone number to her particular worry  was she had a little car and she was it  was registered in a false name and she  was very worried when she went out in  the evening what if it broke down  or she got stopped by the police for  some reason and before she went out she  used to say to me now just tell me again  mum Who am I and I seem to kind of sum  up the whole situation really but all  this publicity actually changed the  public profile of mi5 which now emerged  into the open with an identifiable  leader and so we decided to profit by  this publicity and present a more  up-to-date idea of what mi5 is and what  it really does and what it doesn't do  and counter the negative image that we'd  acquired as a result of never saying  anything at all in public so we invited  the editors of the major newspapers in  for lunch much to their surprise we put  out a booklet explaining the work of a  security service in a democracy and I  gave a high profile lecture on BBC  television the subject is still a very  current subject it was is there a  conflict between security and democracy  and that's a debate that's still going  on in the United Kingdom at the moment  and it did start a very good debate in  the in the sensible newspapers but it  started a not very sensible debate as  well and one journalist wrote that I had  very large ears and that these must be  very useful in covert communication so  after so much change what lies ahead in  the security field well as I've said it  looks as though terrorism from al Qaeda  and associated groups will continue and  unfortunately escalate possibly until  some answer is found to some really  difficult issues the israel-palestine  situation in particular the extreme  inequalities in education and economic  situation between countries the lack of  integration of minority groups in our  cities particular in the UK the Iraq war  and to a lesser extent the war in  Afghanistan I think all that created  this chronic crisis between parts of  Muslim youth and the West so we're now  facing a complex problem but our  intelligence services and our police  forces will get better at dealing with  it and they are doing all the time  thank goodness though it's a  but I no longer have to