welcome everyone to the International  Spy Museum we are delighted to have you  here this evening and I think you are in  for a delightful evening my name is  Peter earnest I'm the executive director  of the museum I'm very honored to have  all of you here this evening but there's  one or two guests I would particularly  like to invite you to meet and one and  it's such a pleasure because it is a  rare visit not all that common I would  like you to meet the founder of the  International Spy Museum  Milt mauls and his wife Tamar who is the  chairman of our board none of this would  have happened if mild' hadn't had the  idea he served in NSA as a young man and  had the idea of doing something like  this for many years and he did it the  other one I would like you to meet is a  member of our board former chief of  counterintelligence for the Soviet KGB  general Oleg Kalugin yes  and our guest this evening of course as  you know you have come here is Dame  Stella Rimington Dame Stella Rimington  was spent some 35 years in the British  security service from 1969 to 1996  during that period she served in all  three branches both counter-intel Kenner  espionage counter subversion and  counterterrorism and rose to become head  of each of those staffs she then was the  direct the Deputy Director one of the  deputy directors and following a trip  she made to Moscow the first trip that  the British had made to Moscow to meet  the KGB under other circumstances on a  return she was told that she'd been  promoted to director general of mi5 the  British domestic security service she  was the first woman promoted to that  position she was also the first head of  mi5 to be publicly announced the press  had been trying to get a picture of her  for months well they finally got one  because she posed for it  Dame Remington was a great advocate of  openness and transparency transparency  to the public and openness as to the  nature of the service she was the  proponent of that when she was there and  she continued to do that in her  retirement both in her memoirs her  autobiography open secret as well as  presentations speeches and so forth that  she's made subsequently the year that  she retired 1996 in the Honours List for  that year she was named Dame Stella  Rimington commander order of the bath  and that is a title that she has that is  the DCB to this day in 2004 she  published her first novel at risk her  second one in 2006 secret asset and in  2007 illegal action which is the one  that we have here this evening and will  be available for signing after the  presentation  subsequent to her retirement she served  on several boards one of which was of  course Marks &amp; Spencer which are all  familiar with the BG group and I'm very  very honored to say the board of the  International Spy Museum since 2007 we  are indeed very honored to have her on  the board before naming into the board  we of course did a background check she  did very well  I of course asked my colleagues because  you know there's there's the official  things that are said and there's what  people say around the water fund water  fountain and everybody I know gave her a  real thumbs up and I think what finally  solidified it for me was after I wrote  Dame Stella Remington I said dear Dame  Stella Rimington she wrote back a very  cordial reply and at the end of it she  said call me Stella  please join me in welcoming Stella  Rimington  well good evening everybody I'm  delighted to be here at the  International Spy Museum which i think  is one of the most interesting and  lively museums in town but then i would  say that wouldn't as i'm on the board as  you've heard i'm going to talk about my  experiences of my career in mi5 and  really i'm going to talk about how the  threats that that service faces have  changed and how mi5 had to change as  well to cope with them  I joined mi5 as you've heard in the late  1960s and the fact that I joined at all  was a complete fluke because in those  days nobody knew anything about British  intelligence nobody knew who worked  there nobody knew what it was where it  was and in particular of course nobody  knew how you joined and recruitment like  everything else was a secret as I found  out now the website setting out the sort  of jobs that there are available and you  can apply online and they have job  advertisements appearing in the  newspapers and even on the notice boards  of gyms I hear and surprisingly enough  if you google Russian intelligence  service you get a sponsored link for  jobs in mi5  and I think you probably know there's a  very popular TV series which is called  mi5 here and spooks in the United  Kingdom though I must say that I  wouldn't rely on it too much as a source  of information  I was actually recruited to mi5 in India  I'd given up my first profession which  was as a historical archivist to go with  my husband who is a diplomat and he was  posted to the British High Commission in  New Delhi and there I was cheerfully  doing what diplomats wives did in those  days which I must say was not very much  except going to coffee mornings and  appearing in amateur dramatics when  somebody sidled up to me in the British  High Commission compound and tapped me  on the shoulder and said do you want to  be a spy or something like that anyway  and it was the mi5 man in charge of the  small little sort of department which  they had there in those days and he  recruited me as a part-time member of  mi5 as a clerk typist the only  difficulty was that with a degree as I  had an opposed graduate diploma I  couldn't actually type but that didn't  really seem to matter because in those  days people were recruited really for  who they were rather than what they  could do and of course as a diplomat's  wife I was by definition reliable I  thought she's wonderful I knew nothing  at all about mi5 except that I knew that  it had something to do with spies and  there was a lot of spying going on in  those days by both sides in India it was  one of the four fronts really of the  Cold War and I found this absolutely  fascinating so when we came home I  applied for and got a full-time job in  mi5 the UK has got three intelligence  services for those of you who don't know  there's mi5 mi6 and GCHQ  and they're all civilian none of them  have any police powers or any military  powers and none of them are exactly like  any aspect of US intelligence mi5 the  defensive arm and their job is to defend  the country against major threats to our  national security whatever they may be  and obviously they very  on the state of the world at any given  time when I joined the major threat came  from espionage from the Soviet Union and  it's our lives nowadays of course the  major threat comes from terrorists mi5  works very closely with mi6 which is our  foreign intelligence gathering service  which equates broadly but not totally  with the CIA and then we have GCHQ which  equates much more closely with NSA and  together these three services gather  intelligence from many different sources  at home and abroad by all the classical  intelligence gathering means by  intercepting communications  eavesdropping with covert microphones in  the places where people meet to do their  plotting surveillance physically  following people around but the best  sources even in today's technical world  are human beings the terrorists or the  spy deep in the heart of their  organization who can be persuaded to  change sides stay where they are and  work for you effectively to betray their  colleagues and finding those people and  recruiting them and running them is  still an essential part of intelligence  work then of course you've got carefully  to analyze and assess everything that  you've learned decide if you believe it  go out and get more and take action and  this is the key thing at the right  moment you hope to prevent the bomb  going off or the spy getting the secrets  intelligence will never give you the  whole picture by definition so it's  often extremely difficult to decide what  action to take and when to take it in  the Cold War much action came in the  form of disruption try and identify the  foreign intelligence officer who's  working against you in your country try  and recruit them to your side or if you  can't expose them embarrass them get  them expelled from the country nowadays  when terrorists are the main threat  action is much more likely to be in the  form of arrests but arrests will not be  effective unless you've got evidence  that you can use  in court and often of course  intelligence isn't evidence and you  can't use it in court so the dilemma is  acute if you take action too late the  bomb goes off if you take action too  soon you can't hold your suspects and  they get away and may come back another  day when you haven't got any  intelligence at all when we were working  against the IRA as we were for a good  part of my career we tended to let their  operations run on we watched them as  they plotted and planned and really we  watched them write to the moment when  they were planting their bomb and then  they were arrested and of course there  was plenty of evidence now when my  former colleagues are dealing with  suicide bombers with the possibility of  extremely nasty forms of attack the  risks of doing that are deemed to be too  great and the police feel the need to  arrest on the intelligence of intent and  of planning and then of course they've  got to collect evidence afterwards in  order to justify those arrests and bring  these people to court and that's a  problem and that's partly why in the UK  there is a big debate going on at the  moment as the government is seeking  powers to hold suspects in terrorist  cases longer before charging them than  they have been allowed to do in the past  and the other angle to that of course is  the complexity of the data that's now  having to be analyzed as these cases  often have very big international  connections of all kinds so the big  debate goes on between the government  and the supporters of civil liberties  who feel that these powers are not  necessary and breach are hard-won civil  liberties I joined mi5 at the height of  the Cold War an overwhelming stress of  course in those days was from the Soviet  Union and their East European allies and  their efforts at espionage and  subversion as they tried to spread world  communism and undermine Western  democracies their intelligence services  were present in our capitals in large  numbers masquerading as diplomats  journalists Trade Representatives you  name it really  and in those days it was all rather like  a john lecarre a novel for those of you  who read John the Kerry and know about  George Smiley and his colleagues people  were leaving packets of money and hollow  trees on Hampstead Heath in return for  piles of secret documents our jobs  course was to find out what was going on  and stop it and the way we countered  Cold War espionage was through detailed  investigation often over years of the  spy we got results in those days through  my a minut knowledge of the opposing  intelligence services of their people of  the their modus operandi as we called it  so we followed them around we listened  to their telephones we watched their  headquarters in their embassies in our  capitals and we run double agents  against them to try and find out what  they were up to and disrupt them in  those days speed was not nearly as  important as certainty the mi5 I joined  in those days was a static secretive  inward-looking  organization and that attitude was  partly explained by a well-founded fear  of infiltration  I joined not that long after the  Cambridge spies Sylvie Burgess and  MacLean had been exposed and had fled to  Moscow and there were people in British  intelligence and also in the CIA on this  side who were convinced that the whole  of Western intelligence was infiltrated  a